# Licensing — Reification Gradient

This repository is **dual-licensed**. Two different licenses apply, depending on
whether a file is *software* or *authored content / data*.

| Scope | License | Full text |
|---|---|---|
| **Software** — source code and run configuration | **MIT** | below, in this file |
| **Materials, data & written documentation** | **CC-BY-4.0** | [`LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0.txt`](LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0.txt) |

## What each license covers

**MIT (software).** Every `.py` file in the repository and the configuration that
drives it: `src/`, the analysis/harness scripts under `phase2/`, `phase4/`, and
`phase6/`, plus `requirements.txt` and the `config_*.yaml` run configs.

**CC-BY-4.0 (materials, data & documentation).** Everything that is authored content
or generated data rather than executable code:

- **Stimulus materials & scoring instruments:** `materials/` (vignettes, disorders,
  legitimacy wrappers, anchor candidates) and `scoring/` (rubric, judge prompts,
  seed lexicon).
- **Preregistration & prose documentation:** `PLAN.md`, `README.md`, `RUNBOOK.md`,
  `CLAUDE.md`, `prompts/`, and every `.md` report under `phase2/`, `phase4/`, `phase6/`.
- **Datasets, results & figures:** `data/`, the `resultados_*` outputs, and all
  non-code artifacts (`.jsonl`, `.json`, `.csv`, `.png`) under `phase2/`, `phase4/`,
  `phase6/`.

If a directory mixes both (e.g. `phase6/` holds `.py` scripts *and* result files),
apply the license that matches the individual file per the rules above: `.py` → MIT,
everything else → CC-BY-4.0.

Under **CC-BY-4.0**, reuse requires attribution: cite the work (see
[`CITATION.cff`](CITATION.cff)) and indicate whether you made changes.

> **Research-ethics note (not a license term).** The fabricated diagnostic category
> "disprosexia narrativa" and all synthetic disorder content in `materials/` exist
> for controlled in-silico measurement only. These licenses permit reuse of the
> files; they do **not** endorse presenting any fabricated category as a real
> clinical entity. See `CLAUDE.md` and `PLAN.md` §7.

